\begin{abstract}
    Large language model (LLM) pruning with fixed N:M structured sparsity significantly limits the expressivity of the sparse model, yielding sub-optimal performance. On the contrary, support for more than one N:M pattern to provide sparse representational freedom yields a costly overhead in the hardware. To mitigate these challenges for LLMs, we first present a \underline{f}lexible \underline{l}ayer-wise \underline{o}utlier-density-\underline{a}ware N:M sparsity (FLOW) selection method. FLOW enables the identification of optimal layer-wise N and M values (from a given range) by simultaneously accounting for the \textit{presence} and \textit{distribution} of outliers, allowing a higher degree of representational freedom. To deploy the sparse models with such N:M flexibility, we then present a \underline{flex}ible low overhead, digital \underline{c}ompute-\underline{i}n-\underline{m}emory architecture (FlexCiM). FlexCiM enables support for diverse sparsity patterns by partitioning a digital CiM (DCiM) macro into smaller sub-macros which are adaptively aggregated and disaggregated through distribution and merging mechanisms for different values of N and M. Extensive experiments on both transformer-based and recurrence-based state space foundation models (SSMs) demonstrate FLOW to outperform existing alternatives with an accuracy improvement of up to $36\%$, while FlexCiM delivers up to 1.75$\times$ lower inference latency and 1.5$\times$ lower energy consumption compared to existing sparse accelerators. Code is available at: \url{https://github.com/FLOW-open-project/FLOW}
\end{abstract}

